            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.048,
            0.862,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadrilaterals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.562,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "AE = YS (as they are the opposite sides of the parallelogram)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.125,
            0.563,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The angles marked using a single arc are equal, and so are the angles marked using a double arc, since they are alternate angles of parallel lines."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.198,
            0.562,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, by the ASA condition, the triangles are congruent, that is,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.573,
            0.076,
            0.857,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.573,
                0.076,
                0.857,
                0.218
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.236,
            0.286,
            0.252
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { A O E } \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm { Y O S } ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.253,
            0.847,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, OA = OY, and OE = OS, since they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.29,
            0.559,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, O is the midpoint of both diagonals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.319,
            0.553,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is it wrong to write "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  AOE  \\cong \\Delta  SOY?"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Why?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.35,
            0.769,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Property 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.381,
            0.816,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Do the diagonals of a parallelogram intersect at a particular angle?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.423,
            0.726,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.4 Quadrilaterals with Equal Sidelengths"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.458,
            0.608,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Are squares the only quadrilaterals that have equal sidelengths? Let us explore this question through construction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.52,
            0.608,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Draw two equal sides AD and AB, that are not perpendicular to each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.571,
            0.377,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.312,
                0.571,
                0.377,
                0.616
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_15.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.623,
            0.446,
            0.837,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.623,
                0.446,
                0.837,
                0.602
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_16.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.619,
            0.846,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Can we complete this quadrilateral so that all its sides are of the same length?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.662,
            0.538,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mark a point C whose distance from B and D is equal to AB (or AD). To do this, measure AB using a compass. Keeping this length as the radius, cut arcs from B and D."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.547,
            0.646,
            0.879,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 19,
              "bbox": [
                0.547,
                0.646,
                0.879,
                0.789
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_19.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.768,
            0.539,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now we have a quadrilateral with equal sidelengths and one of its angles "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Note that we could have constructed"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.823,
            0.843,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "such a quadrilateral by taking any angle less than "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (in place of 50°)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.847,
            0.848,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A quadrilateral in which all the sides have the same length is a rhombus.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
